{
  "image_name": "02d3d3836125e41769edc3b935f28240.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure contrasts natural cell signaling with several synthetic biology strategies that reprogram immune responses. On the left, native TNF signaling is shown as a layered pathway: extracellular ligand binding to TNFR1 activates intracellular adaptor proteins and kinase cascades, which ultimately drive transcription factors such as NF-κB in the nucleus.\n\nThe central and right portions show how this pathway can be rewired with CRISPR editing and designer promoters. One scheme inserts a synthetic TNFR1 module under an NF-κB–responsive promoter, creating feedback that links TNF detection to engineered gene expression. Additional modules couple signaling to calcium entry through TRPV4 and activation of an IL-1 receptor complex, producing downstream NF-κB signaling and upregulation of targets including PTGS2 and IL1RA.\n\nThe lower panels extend this idea to custom receptors and orthogonal circuits. An anti-uPAR CAR directs T cells to recognize senescent cells and trigger apoptosis while preserving broader T cell functions. Separate platforms use synthetic Notch or MESA-style receptors to sense soluble or presented ligands and convert them into transcriptional outputs, such as expression of sTNFR1 or IL-2. Overall, the figure illustrates a progression from endogenous signaling to programmable receptor–gene circuits for controlled therapeutic responses.",
